{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | sed -n '1314,1435p;1870,1910p;1935,1952p;1998,2022p;2088,2120p;2158,2200p'\"","aggregated_output":"  1314\t  **The module store (r6, amended r7).** A child runs with an\n  1315\t  isolated store, and its exports must not leak that isolation. A\n  1316\t  module that binds `(def x 1)` and exports `(fn [] x)` publishes a\n  1317\t  closure whose free read of `x` would, if only the closure value\n  1318\t  crossed the boundary, resolve through the receiving task's store\n  1319\t  and fall through to its primitives and module registry -- where\n  1320\t  `x` is unbound or bound to something the publisher never saw. The\n  1321\t  stores therefore cross with the exports, under UCF section 7.6.2's\n  1322\t  slice discipline:\n  1323\t  - *Lift.* One snapshot is encoded per module, not just the child's\n  1324\t    own: the child's halted store under its manifest address, and\n  1325\t    for every dependency whose store the exports reach transitively\n  1326\t    (a re-exported closure carries that dependency's `:store-of`),\n  1327\t    that dependency's store instance as it stands in the child's\n  1328\t    state at halt -- including mutations the child made through the\n  1329\t    dependency's own closures, which a snapshot of the child's store\n  1330\t    alone would not carry. A `:store-of` with no snapshot is a lift\n  1331\t    refusal, `:yin.k/non-portable` with\n  1332\t    `:yin.k/kind :missing-module-store`. Each snapshot encodes as a\n  1333\t    `:yin.k/store` slice (UCF 7.6.2); whatever that grammar refuses\n  1334\t    (a host object in the store; engine resources cannot be, by the\n  1335\t    private-resource rule below) refuses the lift and the install.\n  1336\t  - *Marker.* Every closure marker names the store its body resolves\n  1337\t    against, `:yin.k/store-of m` with `m` the module's manifest\n  1338\t    address -- an amendment to UCF section 7.5.1 listed in section\n  1339\t    11, item 12. The snapshots travel once per slice, not per\n  1340\t    closure.\n  1341\t  - *Lower.* Each receiving task instantiates every snapshot into\n  1342\t    `:module-stores {m {sym value}}` in its own state, one instance\n  1343\t    per module: a store the task already holds stands as\n  1344\t    instantiated -- first link wins, and a later slice never\n  1345\t    overwrites a live instance -- while snapshots it lacks are\n  1346\t    instantiated fresh, so a task sees exactly one store per module.\n  1347\t    Nothing is written into any task's ambient store: this is\n  1348\t    7.6.2's \"there is no merge\" rule at the module grain -- a\n  1349\t    receiver binding of the same name can neither supply nor shadow\n  1350\t    the module's value.\n  1351\t  - *Run time.* While a closure carrying `:store-of m` executes, the\n  1352\t    module store of m is its **active store**: free reads resolve\n  1353\t    through it first and the receiver's free env, primitives, and\n  1354\t    registry second; the ambient store does not participate. Direct\n  1355\t    store instructions route by the same context -- `:store-put`\n  1356\t    (and the `:vm/store-put` effect) writes the active module\n  1357\t    store, and `:store-get` reads it with **no ambient fallback**:\n  1358\t    a key the module store lacks is absent, exactly as a top-level\n  1359\t    read of an absent key is; engine resources are unreachable\n  1360\t    either way, by the private-resource rule below. The context\n  1361\t    threads through calls: a frame records the caller's active\n  1362\t    `:store-of` (or none), applying a closure makes that closure's\n  1363\t    `:store-of` active, and returning restores the frame's, so a\n  1364\t    module closure calling another module's closure routes each\n  1365\t    body's reads and writes to its own module. A tail call that\n  1366\t    empties the continuation -- the closure's body was the whole\n  1367\t    computation -- clears the active context with the last frame,\n  1368\t    and the VM admits new top-level input only with no active\n  1369\t    context, which `yin.repl`'s append asserts, so input after a\n  1370\t    module closure applied never routes through a stale module\n  1371\t    store. Sibling exports in one task share one store instance,\n  1372\t    so a write one makes is visible to the next; each task holds\n  1373\t    its own instances, so writes never cross tasks. A declared\n  1374\t    obligation the store does not hold falls through to the\n  1375\t    receiver's bindings exactly as step 5b checked them; a\n  1376\t    conditional definition that ran overwrites the module's own\n  1377\t    slot, which is the publisher's semantics, not a shadow.\n  1378\t  - *In the child.* The same discipline holds transitively: a\n  1379\t    dependency's closure runs against the dependency's store\n  1380\t    instance in the child's state, its mutations are what the lift\n  1381\t    snapshots above, and an export a child re-exports carries that\n  1382\t    dependency's `:store-of`, never the child's ambient bindings.\n  1383\t\n  1384\t  **Private engine resources (r8).** r7 let `:store-get` fall back\n  1385\t  to the ambient store for engine-filed resource cells. That\n  1386\t  fallback was a forging vector: `engine/gensym` mints resource\n  1387\t  keys from the VM's counter (`:stream-0`, `:cursor-1`), the ids\n  1388\t  are predictable, and a linked module could emit\n  1389\t  `[:store-get :stream-0]` and read a host handle no publisher\n  1390\t  handed it. The split is therefore physical, not conventional:\n  1391\t  stream handles, cursor cells, and the FFI pair live in a private\n  1392\t  `:resources` table in VM state; the engine's own machinery\n  1393\t  (wait-set resolution, handle creation and polling, resume) reads\n  1394\t  and writes only that table; and no user store instruction and no\n  1395\t  `resolve-var` step consults it, at top level or inside a module\n  1396\t  closure. The ids may stay predictable -- they name nothing a\n  1397\t  store instruction can reach. This amends the store model of UCF\n  1398\t  sections 4.1 and 7.6.2 (listed with the other amendments in\n  1399\t  section 11, item 12): a migration slice still carries resource\n  1400\t  cells, remapped at resume, but beside the program store, never\n  1401\t  inside it.\n  1402\t\n  1403\t  **Resource lowering (r9).** The continuation format's own decode\n  1404\t  rules move with the split, or the private table is bypassed at\n  1405\t  every boundary crossing: UCF section 7.5.1's table decodes a\n  1406\t  `:yin.k/stream` marker to \"an attached handle, under a fresh\n  1407\t  store key\" and a `:yin.k/cursor-ref` to \"a store cursor entry\",\n  1408\t  and section 7.5.3 lowers each logical cell to \"a fresh store\n  1409\t  cursor entry\". From r9 those decode targets are the private\n  1410\t  table, as an amendment to UCF sections 7.5.1 and 7.5.3 (added to\n  1411\t  the list in section 11, item 12): lowering installs an attached\n  1412\t  handle under a fresh **resource id** in `:resources`, lowers a\n  1413\t  logical cell to a cursor entry in `:resources` seeded with its\n  1414\t  carried position, and remaps every `:yin.k/cursor-ref` to that\n  1415\t  entry; two refs to one cell still share one entry. Program\n  1416\t  values keep only the non-forgeable references -- the opaque\n  1417\t  `stream-ref` and `cursor-ref` ids, exactly as a running program\n  1418\t  holds them -- and no store key is ever created. A module export\n  1419\t  carrying a stream or cursor reference therefore lifts and lowers\n  1420\t  through the same rules: the marker crosses in the slice, the\n  1421\t  receiver's `:resources` gains the attachment or cell, and the\n  1422\t  export's value in the receiver is the remapped reference,\n  1423\t  indistinguishable from one the receiver made itself.\n  1424\t  Executing exports inside the owning child instead was considered and\n  1425\t  rejected: it keeps a child VM alive for the parent's lifetime, and\n  1426\t  every application of an export becomes a function call across a VM\n  1427\t  boundary, which is the coupling the axioms forbid. Relocation costs\n  1428\t  one lift and one lower per install and nothing afterwards, and it is\n  1429\t  the same machinery UCF already needs, exercised in-process. The\n  1430\t  offset table it needs is one vector of `[identity offset length]` per\n  1431\t  positional kernel, appended to by `attach-image`; `yin.repl` already\n  1432\t  computes the offset and discards it, so this is recording a fact the\n  1433\t  loader has in hand, not a new computation. `attach-image` itself is\n  1434\t  the one new kernel operation this section asks for, and it is the\n  1435\t  append half of an operation `yin.repl` already performs.\n  1870\t`:unresolved-free`; a shadowed export name in the requiring program's\n  1871\tstore is `:shadowed-free`; a free name the manifest never declares is\n  1872\t`:undeclared-free`; two outstanding requires on one response stream\n  1873\teach restore on their own id; a response landing before the entry would\n  1874\thave been polled is not skipped; a module requiring another links\n  1875\ttransitively while a third task keeps running; a require cycle is\n  1876\t`:require-cycle`; a module that binds a name and exports a closure\n  1877\treading it links, the closure reads the module's value in the\n  1878\tparent, a parent binding of the same name neither supplies nor\n  1879\tshadows it, and a write from one export is visible to the next\n  1880\tapplication in the same task and to no other task; a read inside a\n  1881\tbody applied before its definition retains its obligation and is\n  1882\t`:undeclared-free` unless the manifest declares it; an export-only\n  1883\timage with no application site produces no obligations; a parked\n  1884\tentry recorded before an attach restores after it at the same pcs\n  1885\twith `:segment` never assigned from the entry; a register call in\n  1886\tflight while a nested require attaches an image returns into the\n  1887\tgrown code space, the attachment intact after the return; two tasks\n  1888\tholding images of different lengths require the same module and each\n  1889\tapplies its export correctly; a `:store-put` executed inside an\n  1890\texported closure writes the module store, and a module closure\n  1891\tcalling another module's closure writes each module's own store; a\n  1892\tslice re-exporting a dependency closure carries the dependency's\n  1893\tstore snapshot with the child's mutations, and a task already\n  1894\tholding that dependency's store keeps its own instance; a module\n  1895\tforging a predictable resource key (`[:store-get :stream-0]`)\n  1896\tagainst a task with a live stream reads nothing on any backend; a\n  1897\tmodule export carrying a stream or cursor reference lifts and\n  1898\tlowers, the attachment or cell landing in the receiver's private\n  1899\t`:resources` and only the remapped reference in program values;\n  1900\tREPL input following a module closure's tail-completed application\n  1901\tdefines into the task's own store, no stale module context; a\n  1902\twalker closure whose body node is shared by two `:lambda` rows\n  1903\twith different params lifts from its recorded source row and\n  1904\trefuses `:unrooted-body` when a fabricated `[node params]` pair\n  1905\tmatches no row; `(vm :stack)` and `(vm :register)` in `yin.repl` link\n  1906\tthe same manifest by H and R respectively and produce B0-equal results.\n  1907\t\n  1908\t**M5. `yin.repl` wiring.** The REPL composes the link pair per VM and\n  1909\tthe content pair per connection (`connect` already opens the RPC\n  1910\tclient). `(require 'foo)` at the prompt exercises the whole path.\n  1935\t          src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc, semantic.cljc,\n  1936\t          debruijn/stack.cljc, debruijn/register.cljc\n  1937\t            (attach-image; offset table and image\n  1938\t             identities on the positional kernels; row index\n  1939\t             on the walker; closures carry :store-of; the\n  1940\t             register frame drops :segment and :hash; store\n  1941\t             routing and its frame threading)                      (M4)\n  1942\t          src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc  (append-*-image over attach-image;\n  1943\t                                   the four streams)          (M4, M5)\n  1944\t          docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md\n  1945\t            (section 7.4.1 rows, 7.4.3 variants, 7.5.1 marker\n  1946\t             keys :yin.k/binding and :yin.k/store-of, 7.5.1 and\n  1947\t             7.5.3 resource decode targets)                        (M4)\n  1948\tDepends:  dao.jing (segment-key, segment-matches?, materialize!)\n  1949\t          dao.jing.remote, dao.jing.remote.step, dao.stream.rpc\n  1950\t          yin.vm (validate-rows, free-names, primitive-profiles)\n  1951\t          yin.vm.code (well-formed-vector?)\n  1952\t          yin.vm.ledger (derive-record, verify-derivation)\n  1998\t\n  1999\tRequire:\n  2000\t\n  2001\t10. `(require 'foo)` on each of the four backends, over ring buffers,\n  2002\t    parks, links, installs through the child phases, and resumes with\n  2003\t    `'foo`; the same over the JVM WebSocket content path.\n  2004\t11. The scheduler registry holds no function that arrived by linking\n  2005\t    and no lowered bindings, only the portable slice; every task-local\n  2006\t    `:bindings` was lowered from a slice an isolated child evaluation\n  2007\t    produced, which reached halt with every export bound.\n  2008\t12. UCF section 7.4.1 carries `:link-request`, `:link-response`, and\n  2009\t    `:install` safepoints, section 7.4.3 their pending variants, section\n  2010\t    7.5.1 the `:yin.k/binding` closure variants and `:yin.k/store-of`\n  2011\t    key of section 7.3, sections 4.1, 7.5.1, 7.5.3, and 7.6.2 the\n  2012\t    private engine-resource table of section 7.3 and the resource\n  2013\t    decode targets it moves out of the store, and a parked entry of\n  2014\t    each kind lifts and lowers through UCF's round-trip test.\n  2015\t13. Every swapped-image and swapped-name case in M4 refuses before load.\n  2016\t14. The assertion policy of section 8.2 refuses `:ambiguous-name`,\n  2017\t    discards unproven assertions as `:unauthenticated`, and ignores\n  2018\t    undeclared principals, at a fixed snapshot.\n  2019\t15. A module that defines a name and then reads it in the main\n  2020\t    sequence produces no obligation for that name; a main-sequence\n  2021\t    read before the definition is `:use-before-definition`; a read\n  2022\t    inside a body is free of obligation only when an unconditional\n  2088\t    dependency's store transitively carries that dependency's\n  2089\t    snapshot, including a mutation the child made through the\n  2090\t    dependency's closure, a missing snapshot is\n  2091\t    `:yin.k/non-portable` with `:yin.k/kind :missing-module-store`,\n  2092\t    and a receiving task that already holds the dependency's store\n  2093\t    keeps its own instance; a module closure calling another\n  2094\t    module's closure routes each body's reads and writes to its own\n  2095\t    module; in the child, a closure re-exported from a dependency\n  2096\t    reads that dependency's module store, not the child's ambient\n  2097\t    bindings; a module emitting `[:store-get :stream-0]` against a\n  2098\t    task with a live stream at that id reads nothing on any\n  2099\t    backend, because engine resources live in the private table no\n  2100\t    store instruction reads; a module export whose captured\n  2101\t    environment carries a stream or a cursor reference lifts and\n  2102\t    lowers: the receiver's private `:resources` gains the\n  2103\t    attachment or cell, the export's value is the remapped\n  2104\t    reference, and no store key is created; after a module closure\n  2105\t    applied at the\n  2106\t    REPL and its tail call completed, new top-level input defining\n  2107\t    a name of its own binds the task's store, never the module's;\n  2108\t    a walker closure whose body node sits under two `:lambda` rows\n  2109\t    with different params lifts from its recorded source row --\n  2110\t    annotated on the decoded node at load, copied into the closure\n  2111\t    at evaluation -- and a fabricated closure with no recorded row\n  2112\t    resolves by the `[node params]` index or refuses\n  2113\t    `:unrooted-body`.\n  2114\t\n  2115\t## 12. Open decisions\n  2116\t\n  2117\tRecorded so a later plan does not rediscover them. None blocks M1 to M3;\n  2118\tthe first concerns material the composition supplies to a policy section\n  2119\t8.2 fixes.\n  2120\t\n  2158\t  scheduler's tasks needs a scheduler-owned cell and an effect\n  2159\t  discipline for writing it; that is not this document's mechanism.\n  2160\t\n  2161\t## 13. Reconciliation record\n  2162\t\n  2163\t### 13.1 Revision r9 (round 8, `gpt-6-sol`)\n  2164\t\n  2165\tThe round-8 review closed the walker-provenance item (no action);\n  2166\tone partial finding remained, the last open item. The round-8\n  2167\tfindings file was not present in `collab/`; r9 worked from the\n  2168\titem in the task:\n  2169\t\n  2170\t| Item                              | Resolution                        |\n  2171\t|-----------------------------------|-----------------------------------|\n  2172\t| r8 put engine handles in private  | Agreed. UCF 7.5.1 decodes         |\n  2173\t| `:resources`, but the UCF decode  | `:yin.k/stream` and               |\n  2174\t| clauses still installed handles   | `:yin.k/cursor-ref` into store    |\n  2175\t| and cursor cells under store keys | keys and 7.5.3 lowers a cell to a |\n  2176\t| -- bypassing the table at every   | \"fresh store cursor entry\". From  |\n  2177\t| boundary crossing                 | r9 the decode targets are the     |\n  2178\t|                                   | private table: lowering installs  |\n  2179\t|                                   | an attachment under a fresh       |\n  2180\t|                                   | resource id, lowers a logical     |\n  2181\t|                                   | cell to a `:resources` cursor     |\n  2182\t|                                   | entry seeded with its position,   |\n  2183\t|                                   | remaps every `:yin.k/cursor-ref`, |\n  2184\t|                                   | and creates no store key; program |\n  2185\t|                                   | values keep only the opaque       |\n  2186\t|                                   | references. Recorded as an        |\n  2187\t|                                   | amendment to UCF 7.5.1 and 7.5.3  |\n  2188\t|                                   | (criterion 12); criterion 23 and  |\n  2189\t|                                   | M4 gain lift/lower of an export   |\n  2190\t|                                   | carrying a stream or cursor       |\n  2191\t|                                   | reference (7.3 resource lowering).|\n  2192\t\n  2193\t### 13.2 Revision r8 (round 7, `gpt-6-sol`)\n  2194\t\n  2195\tThe round-7 review closed the grow-only-returns item (no action)\n  2196\tand left two partial findings. The round-7 findings file was not\n  2197\tpresent in `collab/`; r8 worked from the two items in the task.\n  2198\tEach, and what r8 did:\n  2199\t\n  2200\t| Item                              | Resolution                        |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
